  "findings": [
    "The generic Dutch Sociology track and current programme’s focus on Dutch society support retaining the Urban Issues major independently of UCR fit. It is the first listed major, directly continues urban inequality, exclusion and welfare-state questions, and is verified in adopted Appendix 1d. The two English-language major priorities remain legitimate programme interests. No current registry identity or language is silently changed. The old social-behavioural-sciences OER URL returned 404; replaced that stale link with the current official OER index and adopted appendix.",
    "The twelve first-year units total sixty credits with their actual three/five/six/eight-credit weights. Year 2 has thirty-six common credits and four six-credit Urban Issues subjects, including the specific twelve-credit Urban Issues research-project variant 400153-B-12. Parent major and project aggregates are not added to their children. The public page lists GROW at zero credits; it is not added to degree totals.",
    "The non-exchange final year has a compulsory twelve-credit placement, eighteen open credits, twelve restricted credits, twelve-credit major-specific thesis and six-credit Social Philosophy. The formal footnote clarifies that the eighteen open credits may combine a minor and/or electives; no named minor is imposed. The restricted twelve-credit block must be instantiated. Selected Global Migration 424019-B-6 and Sociology of Work 424022-B-6, both permitted six-credit subjects from the non-selected Social Risks major. They coherently extend urban inequality, migration and labour questions before UCR placement is reconsidered.",
    "The normal thesis is the Spring Urban Issues variant 400998-B-12. The adopted admission rule requires all propaedeutic subjects completed AND sixty postpropaedeutic EC. This is stronger than merely any 120 credits; completing the full first and second years meets it. The catalogue’s general 120-credit wording is read with the stricter adopted condition. Autumn thesis admission is exceptional, not used in this pathway. The thesis is an individual empirical sociological article based primarily on suitable secondary data, with proposal, peer feedback and symposium presentation.",
    "The placement requires at least 336 hours in a relevant organisation. It can be full-time over about eight-and-a-half weeks or part-time over a semester/year. The organisation supervises in consultation with the coordinator. An approved learning plan and signed contract are mandatory BEFORE starting; a reflective final report is assessed pass/fail. No host is fabricated. A feasible non-exchange arrangement places the flexible placement and Migration (Block 2) in Autumn, and the normal thesis, Work and Social Philosophy (Block 4) in Spring, with twelve open credits in Autumn and six in Spring. This is not a named minor or a guaranteed host timetable.",
    "Academic Skills 2 follows 1; Skills 3 follows the earlier skills and methods while statistics runs alongside. First-year Project 1 then applies the empirical cycle and these foundations. Year-2 qualitative and causal methods precede the later research phase; questionnaires runs alongside the beginning of the Spring project. The project’s knowledge-of-earlier-MTO expectation is not converted into an invented rule that would contradict the published concurrent semester. All earlier-year requirements are completed before the Spring thesis.",
    "All twenty-six selected non-open course entries were retrieved. The Dutch Social Problems entry explicitly directs readers to English counterpart 410101-B-5 for content, which was retrieved as a content source without replacing the Dutch degree component or credits. It emphasises historical framing, problem owners, media and distinguishing social/sociological/policy problems. Questionnaire Construction has sparse English description, but its explicit subject identity, adopted six-credit requirement and SPSS assessment establish the bounded methods relationship; detailed unstated psychometric topics are not invented.",
    "Actual contents govern correspondence: Causal Techniques includes ANOVA, correlation, regression, path/logistic analysis and software interpretation; Life Course Sociology specifically studies health, individual/social determinants and policy across the life course; Inequality focuses on education and labour; Social Philosophy addresses power, group agency/responsibility and social epistemic injustice. These are not reduced to their titles. The placement and individual thesis are not substituted by an unspecified sustainability partner project or generic historical research paper."
  ]
